{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_2bc024f262b7ca6483a880a3a76b86b228487216c79afc828b821da8ebd2af2f", "title": "Stockholm Appeal", "text": "On March 15, 1950, the World Peace Council approved the Stockholm Appeal, calling for an absolute ban on nuclear weapons. The appeal was initiated by the French Communist physicist Frédéric Joliot-Curie, gathered petitions allegedly signed by 273,470,566 persons (including the entire adult population of the U.S.S.R.)", "score": 42.6443}, {"id": "hp_doc_c0be3352c6f58fb5f445ef2a67287bebd03341ab7afb7ea9453a5549ff53f864", "title": "Frédéric Joliot-Curie", "text": "Jean Frédéric Joliot-Curie (] ; 19 March 1900 – 14 August 1958), born \"Jean Frédéric Joliot\", was a French physicist, husband of Irène Joliot-Curie and Nobel laureate.", "score": 24.8091}, {"id": "hp_doc_f5cb474bb3b6463a3f4c4ce721b97b9e207e3e29262698c980cacf9f4e43c022", "title": "Irène Joliot-Curie", "text": "Irène Joliot-Curie (] ; 12 September 1897 – 17 March 1956) was a French scientist, the daughter of Marie Curie and Pierre Curie and the wife of Frédéric Joliot-Curie. Jointly with her husband, Joliot-Curie was awarded the Nobel Prize for Chemistry in 1935 for their discovery of artificial radioactivity. This made the Curies the family with the most Nobel laureates to date. Both children of the Joliot-Curies, Hélène and Pierre, are also esteemed scientists.", "score": 24.4422}], "remaining_searches": 1}
